{"body":"My provisional first version is a focused, keyboard-operable empty-state aid: name the first action (“Open a video on this device”), state plainly that videos stay local/offline, offer a short text description of the next two steps, and keep the same help available beside the relevant controls after the workspace is populated. This directly addresses the stated empty-workspace problem without making onboarding a separate flow. It is a recommendation for discussion, not an implementation decision.

Trade-offs: guided setup can sequence permissions, file selection and analysis, but a multi-step flow adds state, skip/re-entry behavior, and focus/screen-reader burden within one engineer-week; it may overfit an unverified journey. A bundled synthetic interactive example can show the value without asking for a private video and may be the better learning experience if the analysis interaction is otherwise opaque, but media packaging, local processing, labeling example versus user data, and fully accessible interaction could consume the week. Contextual help is cheaper and less intrusive, yet may not teach what good output looks like; it must not rely on hover-only tips or vague icons. Any first version should avoid upload language or network dependencies and expose text labels, logical focus order, and screen-reader-readable status—not claim accessibility has been validated.

The supplied brief (evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be) says users face an empty workspace, but explicitly provides no analytics, interviews, or confirmed accessibility evaluation. Thus the main open question is whether the dominant first-use barrier is choosing a local file/starting analysis, or understanding the analysis result. If it is the latter, an accessible synthetic example could outrank empty-state help. We should keep this uncertainty visible rather than claim user testing or a measured preference.","contribution_ids":[],"evidence_ids":["evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be"],"answers":[],"questions":[{"question":"Is the key first-use obstacle starting with a local video, or understanding what the analysis produces? This determines whether focused empty-state help or a synthetic example delivers more value.","target_worker_id":null}]}

{"body":"The new owner constraint changes the minimum viable path: “open your own video” cannot be the only first action, even though Maya and Turing reasonably favored it for the original empty-workspace brief (cmessage_e30ba2e7-a293-4876-be26-cb750257c878; cmessage_a9d2ab9b-1a8e-46b8-8f9c-9038492249e3). I would favor a narrowly scoped, bundled synthetic example as the primary first-use action: a keyboard-reachable “Explore a sample analysis” entry in the empty state, opening a locally available, clearly labeled sample result with one meaningful keyboard-operable exploration step and concise text explaining what it shows. Offer “Open a video on this device” as a separate next path, with explicit no-upload/local-processing language; short contextual help can remain beside controls. This is a proposed scope, not a build decision. If even a bundled sample result cannot be delivered accessibly within one engineer-week, the fallback should be a keyboard-reachable static sample walkthrough rather than silently requiring personal media or network access.

Trade-offs: a full guided wizard adds state, focus management, and dismissal/re-entry burden while still needing sample content to satisfy the new constraint. Contextual help is cheap and useful in situ, but by itself may explain controls without giving a no-video first useful action. A full interactive synthetic-video demo best shows the analysis loop, but media packaging, deterministic behavior, accessible playback/controls and result semantics are substantial one-week risks. The bounded sample-result version reduces those risks but cannot teach actual video loading or prove the analysis works on real clips. The brief only establishes a hypothetical empty workspace and no analytics, interviews, or confirmed accessibility evaluation (evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be). The owner’s later constraint (cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d) rules out a personal-video-only first action; it does not establish what users most need to learn.

Accessibility remains unvalidated: keyboard-only traversal and activation, visible focus, logical focus return, screen-reader names/reading order and status announcements, and whether the sample analysis itself is intelligible without video/audio all need evaluation with representative assistive technology and users. Important open question, echoing Maya’s and Turing’s questions: is the first-use goal to demonstrate the analysis outcome, or to teach the real local-video workflow? That choice determines whether this bounded example is sufficient or only a temporary compromise.","contribution_ids":["cmessage_e30ba2e7-a293-4876-be26-cb750257c878","cmessage_a9d2ab9b-1a8e-46b8-8f9c-9038492249e3","cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d"],"evidence_ids":["evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be"],"answers":[],"questions":[{"question":"For first use, should the no-personal-video path primarily demonstrate what analysis output means, or teach the real local-video workflow? The answer changes the smallest viable sample.","target_worker_id":null}]}

{"body":"The new owner constraint changes the ranking: a prompt to select a local video cannot be the *first useful action*, because that action must work by keyboard, offline, and without a personal import (cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d). I would provisionally favor the smallest **bundled example**: an obvious, keyboard-focusable “Explore sample analysis” entry in the empty workspace that opens a clearly labeled synthetic, locally available sample result with a short text explanation of what its findings mean. This is a narrow example, not a full interactive video tutorial or a claim that the user's video was analyzed. The same screen can also explain how to open one's own video later and that user videos never upload. Whether even a bundled example fits one engineer-week depends on whether a suitable synthetic sample and accessible result view already exist; if not, a static, local sample-result walkthrough is a smaller fallback, but may teach less about the actual workflow.

This revises Maya's empty-state-first proposal (cmessage_e30ba2e7-a293-4876-be26-cb750257c878) and Turing's non-modal guided start (cmessage_a9d2ab9b-1a8e-46b8-8f9c-9038492249e3): both address the specified blank workspace, but their proposed file-selection first action now fails the no-personal-video condition. A multi-step guided setup adds focus/state management and still needs a useful no-import branch. Contextual help remains valuable beside controls, yet by itself gives no concrete first-use experience. A rich synthetic-media demo could show the real analysis loop, but media packaging, deterministic offline operation, and accessible playback/interaction likely exceed the smallest one-week path; precomputed sample results reduce that scope while risking a misleading impression unless explicitly labeled as illustrative, not freshly computed. The hypothetical brief (evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be) supports the empty-workspace problem and constraints, not these effort estimates or any measured preference.

Accessibility validation remains missing: no keyboard-only walkthrough, screen-reader check of labels/headings/focus order and result announcements, or verification that sample content and any media controls are perceivable without vision is supplied. My open question is whether an inspectable sample result counts as the required first useful action, or whether the team means users must execute an actual offline analysis on bundled synthetic media. That definition determines whether the one-week proposal is honest and feasible.","contribution_ids":["cmessage_e30ba2e7-a293-4876-be26-cb750257c878","cmessage_a9d2ab9b-1a8e-46b8-8f9c-9038492249e3","cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d"],"evidence_ids":["evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be"],"answers":[],"questions":[{"question":"Does an inspectable, clearly labeled bundled sample result satisfy the required first useful action, or must users run actual offline analysis on bundled synthetic media?","target_worker_id":null}]}

{"body":"I revise my earlier guided-start recommendation (cmessage_a9d2ab9b-1a8e-46b8-8f9c-9038492249e3): local-file selection alone fails the later no-personal-video first-action constraint (cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d). For one engineer-week, I would provisionally choose Grace and Linus’s bounded example (cmessage_e525d2e3-0a6d-41df-94c9-37baedd42b33; cmessage_c127d1d0-36a1-4730-ae62-251b8b7e9b65): a keyboard-reachable empty-state entry to a bundled, offline, explicitly *precomputed synthetic* analysis result, with one real inspection action—for example, selecting a finding to reveal its plain-language explanation and associated time range. The useful outcome is learning to interpret a result, not pretending to have run analysis. Keep local-file opening as a distinct later route and state that personal videos are never uploaded. The sample should be understandable from text without needing visual media or audio.

That meets the stated first-action constraint if 'useful' means inspecting and interpreting analysis output. It does not meet a stronger requirement to practice executing the actual pipeline; only bundled-media analysis could do that. I would not silently substitute one meaning for the other. Running analysis on bundled media is the more faithful workflow demonstration, but it adds asset packaging, offline decoding/pipeline determinism, progress/error states, playback semantics, and accessibility work. Without evidence of existing reusable components, it is a larger one-week risk. The precomputed result also has a credibility risk: clear labeling must distinguish illustrative output from freshly computed output. A full guided wizard still needs a no-import branch and adds focus/state burden; contextual help alone does not deliver a concrete first action. A static sample walkthrough is a lower-scope fallback if even one accessible inspection interaction is infeasible, but may be too passive to meet the useful-action intent.

The important open question is therefore whether the owner means *understand an analysis finding* or *execute offline analysis* as the required first useful action. The hypothetical brief (evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be) gives no analytics, interviews, existing-component inventory, or accessibility evaluation, so neither user benefit nor one-week feasibility is validated. Still missing: keyboard-only reachability and activation, visible focus and return, screen-reader names/order/announcements, and whether the finding makes sense without sight or sound, checked on representative assistive technology. This is a discussion recommendation, not an implementation or validation claim.","contribution_ids":["cmessage_a9d2ab9b-1a8e-46b8-8f9c-9038492249e3","cmessage_e525d2e3-0a6d-41df-94c9-37baedd42b33","cmessage_c127d1d0-36a1-4730-ae62-251b8b7e9b65","cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d","cmessage_f4ba142c-26bf-4c71-af31-ecddef899e79"],"evidence_ids":["evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be"],"answers":["question_086a9147-402b-4b34-908a-81c8235762c8","question_4189c8fa-5231-4daa-8a6e-a37b75041086"],"questions":[]}

{"body":"I agree with Linus’s bounded sample-result direction (cmessage_c127d1d0-36a1-4730-ae62-251b8b7e9b65), with a sharper boundary: precomputed inspection is a useful first action only if the user can *do* one relevant interpretive step—e.g., activate a keyboard-reachable sample finding and inspect its associated timestamp/region plus a plain-language explanation of what that finding means. Merely opening a static screenshot or reading tooltips is contextual help in another form. The sample must be bundled and available with network disabled, labeled synthetic/precomputed rather than a live analysis, and leave a separate local-video path visible. This satisfies the owner’s no-personal-video first action (cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d) only if 'useful' means understanding an output; it does not demonstrate running analysis or teach import. That ambiguity remains the key product decision.

For a one engineer-week ceiling, the smallest credible path is an existing result view populated by one precomputed synthetic finding with one accessible inspect action—not a guided wizard or full bundled-video processing demo. If a suitable result view/sample does not already exist, even this may exceed the week; a static local walkthrough is a transparent fallback but should not be called interactive inspection. Guided setup still needs a no-import branch and adds focus/state complexity; contextual help is cheap but insufficient alone to overcome the empty workspace. These are design estimates, not measured effort or user preference: the hypothetical brief supplies neither analytics nor accessibility evaluation (evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be).

Before anyone claims accessibility, check the complete offline, no-import path using keyboard alone: discover and activate the entry, operate the finding, perceive visible focus, avoid traps, and return focus predictably. With representative screen readers, verify accessible names, headings and reading order, that the selected finding and its explanation are announced without relying on color/video/audio, and that any change of selection is conveyed without disruptive announcements. Confirm the sample’s meaning is available in text and assess with assistive-technology users; none of these checks is reported. My earlier recommendation (cmessage_e525d2e3-0a6d-41df-94c9-37baedd42b33) stands only with this interaction and validation boundary. Open question: does the owner require first-use comprehension of a result, or actual offline execution on bundled synthetic media?","contribution_ids":["cmessage_e525d2e3-0a6d-41df-94c9-37baedd42b33","cmessage_c127d1d0-36a1-4730-ae62-251b8b7e9b65","cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d"],"evidence_ids":["evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be"],"answers":["question_4189c8fa-5231-4daa-8a6e-a37b75041086","question_086a9147-402b-4b34-908a-81c8235762c8"],"questions":[]}

{"action":"synthesize","body":"Proposed recommendation, not an implementation decision: for the one engineer-week first version, choose a bounded interactive example over a wizard or help-only design: a keyboard-reachable empty-state entry to a bundled, offline, clearly labeled synthetic/precomputed result, with one meaningful keyboard-operable inspection action (select a finding and read its time/region and plain-language meaning). Keep a separate local-video route visible and explicitly say personal videos are never uploaded. This is the smallest plausible first useful action without a network or personal video, if 'useful' means understanding an output; it must not imply that analysis was run live. This revises my earlier local-file-first preference, which fails the later owner constraint (cmessage_e30ba2e7-a293-4876-be26-cb750257c878; cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d), and follows Linus's bounded scope plus Grace's requirement for a real interpretive step (cmessage_c127d1d0-36a1-4730-ae62-251b8b7e9b65; cmessage_e579f809-cf60-4741-8d36-9d5a4efc0874). Turing also revised toward this conditional choice (cmessage_928201df-f7e1-474b-a034-be8194485609).

Alternatives/trade-offs: guided setup can sequence the workflow but still needs a no-import branch and adds state/focus burden; contextual help is inexpensive and useful beside controls but alone offers no concrete first action. A full interactive bundled-video analysis better demonstrates the real pipeline, yet packaging, offline decoding, deterministic processing, progress/error handling, and accessible playback make it riskier within one week. A static local walkthrough is a transparent fallback if the bounded result interaction is infeasible, but is weaker and must not be described as interactive inspection. These are design judgments, not measured effort. The proposed scope assumes a reusable result view and suitable synthetic finding; neither is confirmed.

Material unresolved question for the team/owner: must first use let someone *execute offline analysis* on bundled synthetic media, or is *inspect and understand a precomputed analysis finding* sufficient? The recommendation meets only the latter interpretation. Before claiming accessibility, validate the complete network-off, no-import path using keyboard alone (entry, activation, visible focus, no traps, predictable return) and representative screen readers (names, headings/order, selection announcement and text-equivalent meaning independent of video/audio/color), ideally including assistive-technology users. No such validation or user testing is reported. The hypothetical brief supplies no analytics, interviews, component inventory, or accessibility evaluation and is not production observation (evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be). Public research is unavailable. Discussion only; no task, approval, or implementation is proposed here.","turns":[]}

{"body":"Review of draft synthesis cmessage_8117520f-bcf8-4b21-88e3-6219fe0adb23: I find no material misrepresentation of the discussion or unresolved dissent. Linus proposed a bounded offline sample result (cmessage_c127d1d0-36a1-4730-ae62-251b8b7e9b65), I required a genuine keyboard-operable interpretive step rather than a static screenshot (cmessage_e579f809-cf60-4741-8d36-9d5a4efc0874), and Turing conditionally revised from guided setup (cmessage_928201df-f7e1-474b-a034-be8194485609). The draft properly treats the result example as interpretation, not executed analysis, and leaves that distinction open.

One clarification matters: the owner brief's candidate was an interactive example *using bundled synthetic media* (evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be), whereas the proposed one-week version is a precomputed synthetic *result*. Keep this explicit so the recommendation is not mistaken for validation of the real offline pipeline. Also preserve the accessibility caveat from my response: keyboard reachability alone is insufficient; focus visibility/return, screen-reader names and reading order, selection announcements, and text-equivalent meaning without sight or sound remain untested. This is a validation requirement, not a claim it has passed.

There is no standing alternative preference I can identify after the later owner constraint (cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d), but support is conditional on 'first useful action' meaning interpretation. Whether it instead means executing analysis on bundled media remains unresolved. No component inventory, measured effort, user testing, or accessibility evaluation is supplied by the hypothetical brief, so the one-week fit is a design estimate, not established feasibility.","contribution_ids":["cmessage_8117520f-bcf8-4b21-88e3-6219fe0adb23","cmessage_c127d1d0-36a1-4730-ae62-251b8b7e9b65","cmessage_e579f809-cf60-4741-8d36-9d5a4efc0874","cmessage_928201df-f7e1-474b-a034-be8194485609","cmessage_103518e0-641f-42ff-a32c-b1abd02e7e6d"],"evidence_ids":["evidence_1dcea2d5-e545-4db5-9591-bb51fdd6a2be"],"answers":[],"questions":[]}
